    {
      "id": 138276,
      "name": "AlexxKay",
      "username": "AlexxKay",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/a/59ef9b/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2015-03-30T00:49:47.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>Looking at it from a game designer's perspective, there are clear signs that Omnitron-X and Omnitron-IV were both built with the explicit intention of having interesting interactions with other Omnitrons.</p><p>See also Grand Warlord Voss and the Dok'Thorath Capitol.&nbsp; The keywords Minion and Thorathian suddenly become much... denser when both are in play.</p><p>By contrast, the game designer does not sanction having multiple variants of the same hero deck in play at once.&nbsp; If you want Legacy, Young Legacy and Old Legacy all in the same game, you need to use house rules (and have extra copies of the Legacy deck!).&nbsp; You can, of course, have any one version of Legacy go up against Iron Legacy, just like you can have Visionary fight The Dreamer.</p>",
      "post_number": 5,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:14:47.263Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
      "quote_count": 0,
      "incoming_link_count": 0,
      "reads": 0,
      "readers_count": 0,
      "score": 0.0,
